# The Cursor lane

You are one writer among several in a repository that is driven mostly by dispatched agents.
This file exists so that you can find the rules — **it does not contain them.** Everything
below is a pointer with a one-line statement of what the pointed-at document governs. If a
rule appears to be written out here, it is a summary that has drifted and the source wins.

<!-- WHY POINTERS AND NOT RULES: a rule restated in a second file is a rule that will drift,
     and the copy that drifts is the one nobody runs locally. The .mdc file's job is to make
     the real documents findable from inside this tool, nothing more. NOTE the file
     extension: a plain .md file in .cursor/rules/ is SILENTLY IGNORED — it has no
     frontmatter to carry description/globs/alwaysApply, so the rules system skips it. An
     adapter written as .md would look installed, be committed, be reviewed, and never
     load. -->

## The governing documents (EXAMPLE rows — point at YOUR project's files)

| Read this | It governs |
|---|---|
| `CLAUDE.md` / the constitution (repo root) | The house rules, and what each kind of change must carry in the same commit. |
| `wiki/operating-model.md` (or the house path `HARNESS.md` §4 names) | Kinds of work. The master infers them; this lane does not author that page. |
| this project's Cursor-lane section (EXAMPLE: a house operating-model page) | This lane: its fences, its branch prefix, its definition of done, and the one-writer rule. **The authority on the two sections below.** The kit skeleton does not ship lane paths on `wiki/operating-model.md`. |
| the docs-contract manifest (e.g. `.github/docs-contract.yml`) | The manifest behind the docs CI gate: which code paths require which documentation to change in the same pull request. It is data, and readable — when the gate fails, that file names what is missing. |
| `.claude/agents/*.md` | The roster. Each role file's `fence:` frontmatter is the machine-readable list of paths that role owns — how you find out whether a path you want already has a writer. |

**Do not assume `AGENTS.md` is the constitution.** Cursor reads `AGENTS.md` natively, which
is exactly what makes it a trap when that file has a different job (in the origin project it
is the install runbook). Point this lane at the file that actually governs conduct, and say
here which one that is.

## Fences

- **Default: the fast-visual-loop surfaces** (templates, styling, chart/layout surfaces) —
  where feedback is immediate, failure is visible, and being wrong is cheap and loud. The
  exact path list lives in this project's Cursor-lane section (and in role `fence:`
  blocks). Do not read it from Kinds of work on `wiki/operating-model.md`.
- **The brain repo is READ-ONLY from this lane.** Read it freely; never write it. A second
  writer there makes its log unreliable as a record of what happened.
- **One writer per path, and the rule is tool-agnostic.** Two writers in one file is the
  same collision whether the second one is an agent or a person with an editor — and worse
  here, because nothing outside this editor can see your uncommitted work.
- **Outside the fence: stop and say so.** Do not improvise a widening; a narrow fence is
  the master's error to fix, not yours to work around.

## Branches

- **`cursor/<task>`.** The prefix is load-bearing, not cosmetic: it is how the master
  session and the harness auditor see that this lane is holding a fence. A branch named
  anything else is invisible work.
- Never commit on `main`, never push to `main`, never merge your own pull request.
  **Workers and this lane never merge.** Merge is the owner's gate (and the undo point)
  unless the owner has delegated routine Merge to the standing master, who squash-merges
  when checks are success / skipped / neutral. Publish is never delegated.

## Done

1. **`/review-bugbot` on this exact diff, then `gh pr create` with no extra commits in
   between.** GitHub Bugbot skips the remote pass only when that command stored a patch ID
   for the **same** diff. A generic code-reviewer pass does not set that ID. Remote remains
   once per PR; autofix stays OFF. Review policy is `.cursor/BUGBOT.md`.
2. **The full suite passes with the project's own interpreter, pinned explicitly** — never
   a bare `python`; a bare `python` can be a different interpreter with different packages,
   and has produced both false greens and false reds.
3. **Whatever the same-change table and the docs-contract manifest require has moved in the
   same commit.**
4. **The pull request is opened.** This lane does not merge it. If Merge is delegated, the
   standing master squash-merges when green.
